ARM R3 FOR #1292 — THE GATE RUNS IT (brief-C rev C's REQUIRED N-1288-2 regression arm; brief: /Volumes/DevMASTER/WEDNESDAY/2_Project_Files/local-model/night/briefs/KS-1341/brief-C.md, section "The failing case", "Arm R3"). ARM-R3-PROD-ONLY-LOG: in YOUR worktree at head, replace the byte-unique line inside fail500 (the drafter READ it once, at webhooks.ts:563) `  logger.error(context, { error: err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err) });` with the brief's tamper `  if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production') logger.error(context, { error: err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err) });` (2 leading spaces, no braces — run the brief's bytes). Predict first, then run the WHOLE ks1341c file: both `RED KS-1341 C1` rows must go RED, each failing at its `development` iteration with `calls: []` where one call is expected and the failure naming `nodeEnv: 'development'`; both C2 rows stay GREEN (production only); C3 SOURCE stays GREEN (the call sites are unchanged); every control stays GREEN. IF ANY C1 ROW STAYS GREEN UNDER R3, THE PER-ENVIRONMENT CLEAR IS LOST — that is a FAIL of #1292's red proof. Run the SAME tamper under YOUR runtime probe too: your probe must red in development, test and unset on both routes. Then run brief-C's own tamper arm (`:416` back to the original SITE line: C1 GET /:id/deliveries, C2 GET /:id/deliveries and C3 SOURCE red, all else green). Restore by bytes after each arm. Say plainly whether the KS1344 weakness is closed IN THIS CELL (the part-A A1 retrofit is #1293's, in the sibling kit).

